---
/**
 * RetirementEducationalContent.astro
 * Educational guide explaining retirement projections, compound growth,
 * regular contributions, inflation adjustments, and safe withdrawal rate assumptions.
 */
---

<div class="rec-wrapper" id="how-retirement-projections-work">
  <!-- ─── Section Header ─── -->
  <header class="rec-header">
    <span class="rec-eyebrow font-mono">Educational Guide</span>
    <h2 class="rec-main-title">How Retirement Projections Work</h2>
    <p class="rec-lead">
      Retirement planning involves forecasting how current savings and regular ongoing contributions
      can grow over time through compound investment returns, and how inflation affects your future
      purchasing power.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- ─── 1. Starting With Your Current Savings ─── -->
  <section class="rec-section">
    <h3 class="rec-section-title">1. Starting With Your Current Savings</h3>
    <div class="rec-prose">
      <p>
        Your existing retirement savings form the starting foundation of your projection. The total
        value of your retirement portfolio at your target retirement age depends on five fundamental
        factors:
      </p>

      <div class="rec-table-card">
        <div class="rec-table-header">
          <h4 class="rec-table-title font-mono">Key Factors in a Retirement Projection</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="rec-table-wrap">
          <table class="rec-data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Factor</th>
                <th>Effect on the projection</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Starting savings</strong></td>
                <td>Provides the initial amount available for potential growth</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Contributions</strong></td>
                <td>Add to the projected retirement balance</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Investment return assumption</strong></td>
                <td>Influences compound growth over time</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Inflation assumption</strong></td>
                <td>Helps estimate future purchasing power</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Years until retirement</strong></td>
                <td>Determines how long savings and contributions can potentially grow</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        The earlier you begin and the larger your starting balance, the more time your money has to benefit
        from long-term compounding.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 2. The Power of Compound Growth ─── -->
  <section class="rec-section">
    <h3 class="rec-section-title">2. How Compound Growth Works</h3>
    <div class="rec-prose">
      <p>
        Compound growth occurs when the investment returns generated by your portfolio are reinvested to
        generate their own returns in subsequent years. Over decades, compounding often generates more
        wealth than the actual cash you contributed.
      </p>

      <div class="rec-concept-card">
        <span class="rec-concept-badge font-mono">Compounding Principle</span>
        <p class="rec-concept-text">
          If you invest <strong>$10,000</strong> at an assumed <strong>7%</strong> annual return, it earns <strong>$700</strong> in Year 1 (ending balance: $10,700). In Year 2, the 7% return applies to the new $10,700 balance, earning <strong>$749</strong>. Over 30 years without any additional contributions, that single $10,000 investment would grow to more than <strong>$76,000</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Important Note on Market Returns:</strong> An assumed annual return rate (such as 6% or 7%) is a
        long-term average for modeling purposes. In real-world financial markets, investment returns fluctuate
        from year to year with periods of gains and losses rather than delivering a constant, smooth return.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 3. The Impact of Regular Contributions ─── -->
  <section class="rec-section">
    <h3 class="rec-section-title">3. The Role of Regular Contributions</h3>
    <div class="rec-prose">
      <p>
        While initial savings provide a starting base, consistent monthly contributions are the primary
        engine of retirement accumulation for most savers.
      </p>
      <p>
        The calculator models monthly contributions as an <em>ordinary annuity</em> compounded at an effective
        monthly rate:
      </p>
      <ul class="rec-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Contribution Consistency:</strong> Regular monthly additions benefit from dollar-cost averaging, buying assets across different market cycles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Duration of Savings:</strong> Contributions made in your 20s and 30s have decades to compound, meaning early dollars do significantly more heavy lifting than dollars contributed close to retirement.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Incremental Increases:</strong> Even small increases in your monthly savings amount (such as an extra $50 or $100 per month) can add tens of thousands of dollars to your final portfolio value over a 20- to 30-year horizon.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For a practical framework on setting personal savings targets and timelines, read our guide on <a href="/blog/retirement-savings-guide">how much to save for retirement</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 4. Inflation Adjustment & Purchasing Power ─── -->
  <section class="rec-section">
    <h3 class="rec-section-title">4. Understanding Inflation &amp; Real Purchasing Power</h3>
    <div class="rec-prose">
      <p>
        A large future dollar figure can be misleading if you do not account for inflation. Over a 20-, 30-,
        or 40-year retirement timeline, price increases reduce what each dollar can buy.
      </p>
      <p>
        The calculator reports two key figures:
      </p>
      <ul class="rec-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Future Portfolio Value (Nominal):</strong> The total dollar balance expected in your account at retirement based on your assumed return rate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Inflation-Adjusted Value (Real):</strong> What that future balance is worth in today's purchasing power, calculated by discounting the nominal value by the cumulative inflation rate over your horizon:
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="rec-formula-card">
        <span class="rec-formula-badge font-mono">Real Return Approximation</span>
        <div class="rec-formula-math font-mono">
          Real Return &approx; ( 1 + Nominal Return ) / ( 1 + Inflation Rate ) - 1
        </div>
        <p class="rec-formula-caption">
          For example, a 7.0% nominal return alongside a 3.0% inflation rate yields an effective real purchasing-power growth rate of approximately 3.88% per year.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Because long-term inflation cannot be predicted with precision, adjusting the inflation rate input allows you
        to test conservative and optimistic economic scenarios.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 5. Retirement Income & Safe Withdrawal Rates ─── -->
  <section class="rec-section">
    <h3 class="rec-section-title">5. Estimating Retirement Income: Withdrawal Rates</h3>
    <div class="rec-prose">
      <p>
        Once you reach retirement, your focus shifts from accumulating wealth to generating sustainable
        living income. The calculator translates your inflation-adjusted nest egg into an estimated
        monthly income using a <strong>4% annual safe withdrawal rate</strong> baseline assumption:
      </p>

      <div class="rec-formula-card">
        <span class="rec-formula-badge font-mono">Estimated Monthly Income Formula</span>
        <div class="rec-formula-math font-mono">
          Estimated Monthly Income = ( Inflation-Adjusted Nest Egg &times; 4% ) / 12
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Understanding the "Safe Withdrawal Rate":</strong> The 4% rule is a widely referenced historical
        guideline suggesting that withdrawing 4% of your initial portfolio in the first year of retirement (adjusted
        annually for inflation) has historically sustained a balanced portfolio over a 30-year horizon.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, no withdrawal rate is universally guaranteed or risk-free. A sustainable withdrawal rate in
        practice depends on several individual factors:
      </p>
      <ul class="rec-list">
        <li><strong>Sequence of Returns Risk:</strong> Market downturns occurring early in retirement can deplete a portfolio faster than downturns later in life.</li>
        <li><strong>Retirement Duration:</strong> Retiring early (e.g., at age 55) requires your portfolio to support 35–45+ years, often requiring a lower withdrawal rate (such as 3.25%–3.5%).</li>
        <li><strong>Asset Allocation &amp; Fees:</strong> The mix of equities, bonds, and cash, combined with investment management fees, affects portfolio longevity.</li>
        <li><strong>Taxes &amp; Other Income Sources:</strong> Government pensions (Social Security, CPP/OAS, Age Pension) and personal pensions reduce the income burden placed solely on your portfolio.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 6. Illustrative Projection Example ─── -->
  <section class="rec-section">
    <h3 class="rec-section-title">6. A Sample Retirement Projection</h3>
    <div class="rec-prose">
      <p>
        Consider a representative scenario modeling 30 years of retirement accumulation:
      </p>

      <div class="rec-example-card">
        <div class="rec-example-header">
          <span class="rec-example-tag font-mono">Illustrative Example</span>
          <span class="rec-example-note font-mono">Not an investment forecast</span>
        </div>
        <div class="rec-example-grid">
          <div class="rec-example-param">
            <span class="rec-param-label">Current Age / Retirement Age</span>
            <span class="rec-param-value font-mono">35 &rarr; 65 (30 Years)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="rec-example-param">
            <span class="rec-param-label">Starting Savings</span>
            <span class="rec-param-value font-mono">$50,000</span>
          </div>
          <div class="rec-example-param">
            <span class="rec-param-label">Monthly Contribution</span>
            <span class="rec-param-value font-mono">$750 / month</span>
          </div>
          <div class="rec-example-param">
            <span class="rec-param-label">Assumed Return / Inflation</span>
            <span class="rec-param-value font-mono">7.0% / 3.0%</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="rec-timeline-table-wrap">
          <table class="rec-timeline-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Metric</th>
                <th>Estimated Value</th>
                <th>Description</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-mono">Total Contributions</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-contrib">$320,000</td>
                <td>$50,000 starting savings + $270,000 total monthly additions</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-mono">Investment Growth</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-growth">$945,712</td>
                <td>Compound interest and investment earnings over 30 years</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-mono">Future Value (Nominal)</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-future highlight">$1,265,712</td>
                <td>Projected account balance at age 65 in future dollars</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-mono">Inflation-Adjusted Value</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-real">$521,452</td>
                <td>Projected balance expressed in today's purchasing power</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-mono">Estimated Monthly Income</td>
                <td class="font-mono text-income highlight">$1,738 / mo</td>
                <td>Based on a 4.0% safe withdrawal rate in today's dollars</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="rec-caption">
        In this 30-year example, compound investment growth ($945,712) accounts for nearly 75% of the total nominal retirement portfolio, demonstrating how compounding accelerates in later years.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 7. Projections Are Estimates ─── -->
  <section class="rec-section">
    <h3 class="rec-section-title">7. Why Actual Results Will Differ</h3>
    <div class="rec-prose">
      <p>
        Retirement calculations are mathematical models designed to help you set targets and evaluate
        scenarios. In the real world, outcomes will differ due to multiple dynamic variables:
      </p>
      <ul class="rec-list">
        <li><strong>Variable Market Performance:</strong> Actual investment returns fluctuate annually with economic cycles and market volatility.</li>
        <li><strong>Changing Inflation &amp; Living Costs:</strong> Healthcare, housing, and general cost-of-living inflation rates change over time.</li>
        <li><strong>Taxation &amp; Account Types:</strong> Withdrawals from tax-deferred accounts (e.g., traditional 401(k), RRSP), tax-free accounts (e.g., Roth IRA, TFSA), or taxable brokerage accounts carry different tax treatments.</li>
        <li><strong>Life Changes:</strong> Career transitions, salary growth, windfalls, emergencies, or changes in retirement age will alter your savings trajectory.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 8. Standardized Disclaimer ─── -->
  <div class="rec-disclaimer" role="note">
    <span class="rec-disclaimer-title font-mono">Disclaimer &amp; Terms of Use</span>
    <p>
      CalcVantage calculators provide estimates for educational and informational purposes only.
      Results may differ from actual financial outcomes and should not be considered financial, investment,
      tax, or retirement advice. Before making any investment, savings, or retirement decisions, consult with a
      licensed financial advisor or certified retirement planning professional.
      For full terms, please review our <a href="/disclaimer">Disclaimer</a> and <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* ── Wrapper ── */
  .rec-wrapper {
    padding: 3rem 0 2rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 2.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  /* ── Header ── */
  .rec-header {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.625rem;
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .rec-eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-mute);
  }

  .rec-main-title {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2.25rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .rec-lead {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-body);
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ── Sections & Prose ── */
  .rec-section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.875rem;
  }

  .rec-section-title {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .rec-prose {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.875rem;
    max-width: 54rem;
  }

  .rec-prose p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-body);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .rec-caption {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-mute);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-top: -0.25rem;
  }

  /* ── Lists ── */
  .rec-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0.25rem 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.625rem;
  }

  .rec-list li {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-body);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-hairline-strong);
  }

  .rec-list li strong {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* ── Table Cards & Data Tables ── */
  .rec-table-card {
    background-color: var(--color-canvas);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  .rec-table-header {
    padding: 0.875rem 1.25rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .rec-table-title {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .rec-table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .rec-data-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    text-align: left;
  }

  .rec-data-table th {
    padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .rec-data-table td {
    padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    color: var(--color-body);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .rec-data-table tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  /* ── Concept & Formula Cards ── */
  .rec-concept-card,
  .rec-formula-card {
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  .rec-concept-badge,
  .rec-formula-badge {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-mute);
  }

  .rec-concept-text {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-body);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .rec-formula-math {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    padding: 0.5rem 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .rec-formula-caption {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-mute);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ── Example Card & Table ── */
  .rec-example-card {
    background-color: var(--color-canvas);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  .rec-example-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 0.875rem 1.25rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .rec-example-tag {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .rec-example-note {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-mute);
  }

  .rec-example-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .rec-example-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .rec-example-param {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.25rem;
  }

  .rec-param-label {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-mute);
  }

  .rec-param-value {
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .rec-timeline-table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .rec-timeline-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    text-align: left;
  }

  .rec-timeline-table th {
    padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .rec-timeline-table td {
    padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    color: var(--color-body);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .rec-timeline-table tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .text-contrib {
    color: var(--color-body);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .text-growth {
    color: var(--color-success);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .text-future.highlight {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .text-real {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .text-income.highlight {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  /* ── Disclaimer ── */
  .rec-disclaimer {
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-warning);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    max-width: 54rem;
  }

  .rec-disclaimer-title {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .rec-disclaimer p {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-body);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .rec-disclaimer a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .rec-disclaimer a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-deep);
  }

  /* ── Mobile responsiveness ── */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .rec-wrapper {
      padding: 2rem 0 1.5rem;
      gap: 2rem;
      margin-top: 2rem;
    }

    .rec-concept-card,
    .rec-formula-card,
    .rec-disclaimer {
      padding: 1rem;
    }

    .rec-example-grid {
      padding: 1rem;
    }

    .rec-timeline-table th,
    .rec-timeline-table td {
      padding: 0.625rem 0.875rem;
    }
  }
</style>
